{{smaller block|With that prynce Must we have ''at do.''
{{right|''Towneley Mysteries'', p. 237.}}}}
{{smaller block|He schalle have ''ado'' every day with hem.
{{right|''Mandeville'', p. 132.}}}}
{{smaller block|I wonder what he had ''ado'' in appearing to me?
{{right|''J. Hogg'', Tales (1837), II. 194.}}}}
'''2.''' In doing; being done.
{{smaller block|Only an eager bustling, that rather keeps ''ado'' than does anything.
{{right|''Earle'', Microcosm., xxvii. 58.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' Doing; action; business; bustle; trouble; labor; difficulty: as, to persuade one with much ''ado.''
{{smaller block|Let’s follow, to see the end of this ''ado.''
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., v. i.}}}}
{{smaller block|We had much ''ado'' to keepe ourselves above water, the billows breaking desperately on our vessel.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Sept. 22, 1641.}}}}
{{smaller block|And what is life, that we should moan? why make we such ''ado?''
{{right|''Tennyson'', May Queen, Conclusion.}}}}

'''-ado.''' [Sp. Pg. ''-ado'', It. ''-ato'' = F. ''é'', < L. ''ātus'', m.: see ''-ate''<sup>1</sup>.] A suffix of Latin origin, the Spanish masculine form of ''-ade''<sup>1</sup>, ''-ate''<sup>1</sup>, as in ''renegado'', ''desperado'', etc. In some words ''-ado'' is an erroneous form of ''-ada'', as in ''bastinado.'' See ''-ada.''

'''adobe''' (a-dō′bā), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Less correctly ''adobi'', colloquially shortened to ''dobie''; < Sp. ''adobe'', an unburnt brick dried in the sun, < ''adobar'', daub, plaster. Cf. ''daub.''] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' The Mexican-Spanish name of the sun-dried brick in common use in countries of small rainfall and of inferior civilization.
{{smaller block|This is a desolate town of two thousand inhabitants dwelling in low dilapidated huts of the most common building material in the Andes — ''adobe'', or sun-dried blocks}}

