2. Specifically, to admit into a relation of affiliation; confer the rights or privileges of kinship upon, as one who is not naturally related or connected; especially, to receive and treat as a child or member of one’s family, etc.: as, the orphans were ''adopted'' by friends. See ''adoption'', 2.—3. To take or receive into any kind of new relationship: as, to ''adopt'' a person as an heir, or as a friend, guide, or example.
{{smaller block|<poem>Titus, I am incorporate in Rome,
A Roman now ''adopted'' happily.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Tit. And., i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|Strangers were very rarely ''adopted'' into a right of property in clan land in the early time.
{{right|''D. W. Ross'', German Land-holding, p. 73.}}}}

